title: Writing documentation
tags: Development
kind: guide
audience: dev
aliases: wiki scripts/doc
summary: The documentation is this wiki — one note per question, plain .tid files under docs/wiki/tiddlers — read, created, renamed and checked with scripts/doc, cited from the code as doc "Title".

The documentation is one wiki, `docs/wiki/`: hand-written notes in
`tiddlers/*.tid`, notes generated from the code in `tiddlers/generated/`, and the
few system notes that configure it in `tiddlers/system/`. It is rendered to HTML and
shown by the GUI's help ([[In-app help]]). When a change alters a behaviour, its
note changes in the same commit. Why it is built this way: [[Why a wiki]].

!! A note

```
title: mf trash restore
tags: Trash [[CLI command]]
kind: reference
summary: Put a trashed entry back at its original path and re-link its metarecord.
aliases: trash:restore

Restores the entry …
```

|!Field |!Values |!Role |
|`title` |free, unique |the note's identity: what links and the code name |
|`tags` |topics and catalogs |a note belongs to the topics it is tagged with; a tag with a space is written `[[Event log]]` |
|`kind` |`overview` `guide` `reference` `rationale` `question` |required, exactly one |
|`summary` |one sentence |required: shown in every list and search result |
|`audience` |`user` (default), `dev` |a `dev` note is about the code, and the help marks it "developer documentation" |
|`status` |`implemented` (default), `deferred`, `proposed`, `record` |what is not built says so; `record` is a design document kept for its reasoning |
|`aliases` |a list of titles |other names the help resolves to this note |

The file is named after the title's identifier (`scripts/doc new` picks it).

!! What goes in a note

* ''One note answers one question'' someone would look for; aim for 20 to 150 lines. A section worth linking to becomes a note.
* Each ''topic'' has an overview note carrying its name, which is the tag of its notes; it stays short — what the topic is, then `<<list-kind guide>>`, `<<list-kind reference>>`, `<<list-kind rationale>>`.
* `guide` says how to do something, `reference` states what is true, `rationale` says why it is so — with the measurement or the incident behind it —, `question` holds one open question.
* Titles are natural English. Only the catalogs follow a convention, so that a title can be guessed: the literal command (`mf trash restore`, `trash:restore`), `<type> panel`, the script's file name, `POST /repos/:repo/query`.
* A command's note does not repeat its generated part — usage, options, keys: it says what the command does and points to the topic's notes.
* Every statement is ''checked in the code'', not copied from an older text.

!! The wikitext that is allowed

Headings (`!!`), paragraphs, lists, tables, code blocks, inline code, `''bold''`,
`//italic//`, `[[links]]`, and the project's procedures. No widget, no raw HTML, no
definition: `scripts/doc check` refuses them outside `system/`.

|!Procedure |!Gives |
|`<<cmd "name">>` |a link to a command's note, in code form |
|`<<key "command" fallback>>` |the key bound to a GUI command — several commands separated by commas —, filled in from the live keybindings by the help; the fallback is the default key |
|`<<list-kind kind>>` |the notes tagged with this one, of that kind |
|`<<tagged-with "tag">>` |the notes tagged with this one and with `tag` — a topic's commands |
|`<<catalog "tag">>` |a catalog's notes, then what the code has and the wiki does not |
|`<<live name>>` |a zone the help fills at display (`grammar`); alone on its line |
|`<<topic-full>>` |every note of the topic, in full |

Traps:

* a list item is ''one line'' — no indented continuation;
* outside code, two hyphens become a dash: a command-line option is always written in backticks;
* a link goes to a note that exists — the check refuses the others. Name what has no note yet in plain text or in code;
* two titles cannot differ by case or punctuation only: they would share an identifier.

!! `scripts/doc`

```
scripts/doc ls [--tag T] [--kind K] [--audience A] [--status S]
scripts/doc show NAME          # a note, by title, alias or identifier
scripts/doc find TEXT          # search, grouped by note
scripts/doc topic NAME         # a topic's note, then every note tagged with it
scripts/doc links NAME         # what it links to, and what links to it
scripts/doc refs NAME          # where the code cites it
scripts/doc new TITLE [--tags L] [--kind K] [--summary S] [--audience A] [--from-gen]
scripts/doc rename OLD NEW     # the note, every link, every tag, every citation in the code
scripts/doc check
scripts/doc gen
scripts/doc build [OUT]
```

Prefer it to grepping the files: `find` and `topic` read titles, tags and aliases.

!! What `check` holds

It runs in `scripts/check.sh` as `wiki` ([[Static checks]]):

* every note has a `kind` and a `summary`, with allowed values, in a file named after its title;
* every link and every tag names a note; no alias collides with a title, an identifier or another alias;
* every note is reachable from [[Documentation]];
* every `doc "Title"` in `crates/` and `scripts/` names a note;
* every key hint names a command that exists, in an invocation a shipped key runs;
* a note tagged with a catalog documents something the code has;
* every item of the code has its note — every catalog is complete, save the exemptions below;
* the generated notes of the shipped scripts are current.

!! Citing a note from the code

A comment points at the note that owns the behaviour with `doc "Title"` — the
title, in double quotes. Any such sequence under `crates/` or `scripts/` counts,
comment or not, and `scripts/doc rename` rewrites them with the note.

!! The generated catalogs

Six catalogs come from the code — [[CLI command]], [[GUI command]], [[Panel]],
[[Shipped script]], [[Reserved field]], [[HTTP endpoint]] — one generated note per item
under `tiddlers/generated/`, holding what the code knows: the usage and options of a
command, its default keys, a field's type, a route's handler. The
hand-written note of the same name says the rest.

`scripts/doc gen` rewrites them. The Rust ones are golden tests: a stale generated
note fails `cargo test` (`doc_gen` in the `cli` and `gui` crates,
`the_wiki_catalog_matches_the_code` in the daemon's — `reserved.rs`, whose registry
`RESERVED_FIELDS` a test holds every `mfr_*` name of the daemon to, and the router). The
endpoints are read from the routers' source, `.route("path", get(handler)…)` by
`.route(…)`, the JavaScript modules at `/__*.js` counted as one, and a `///`
comment on a clap argument ''is'' the help text, so editing one changes a generated
note. `scripts/doc new --from-gen "mf trash restore"` starts a hand-written note
from a generated one: every catalog is enforced, so `check` fails until the note
exists. The one exception is a deferred topic: `$:/mf/config/catalog-exemptions`
lists target prefixes left without a note for now (sync's commands, panel and
endpoints), shown under "Not documented yet" in their catalog. An exemption that
no longer covers anything undocumented fails `check`.

!! Rendering

`scripts/doc build` renders every hand-written note into `docs/wiki/dist/` — one
HTML fragment per note plus `index.json` — which is not committed.
`metafolder-sync-config` installs it under `~/.config/metafolder/docs/`, and refuses
to run before it exists; `scripts/complete-build.sh` does both ([[Building]]). The
GUI serves it at `/docs/`. The tools are tested by `scripts/test-doc-tools.sh`.
